IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fca-nouns`
IMPORT `fca-definitions`
IMPORT `fca-s3-application`

-- Frustrated Contracts Act 1959 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation -- LAYER 1, SECTION 2
-- (adjustment of rights and liabilities of parties to frustrated contracts).
--
-- Section 2 is "subject to section 3", so this module imports the module for section 3 and calls its answer to "does this Act
-- apply to the contract".  Every subsection below then follows s 2(1): if section 2 has no effect in relation to the contract,
-- none of its subsections does (JUST FALSE, carried from the "where" of s 2(1)); if s 2(1) cannot be told, NOTHING.
--
-- THE COURT'S JUDGMENTS.  Where the Act leaves a judgment to the court ("if it considers it just", "as the court considers just",
-- "such sum as appears to be reasonable") the judgment is an INPUT, never computed: a MAYBE BOOLEAN or MAYBE NUMBER where the court
-- may not yet have decided (NOTHING = not yet decided).  What the module does compute is what the Act itself fixes: who may claim,
-- when, and the ceilings ("not being an amount in excess of the expenses so incurred", "not exceeding the value of that benefit").
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST FALSE only where the Act says no in its own words (FALSE-kind comments quote them); NOTHING where the
-- Act does not answer (NOTHING-kind comments say why).  A function that returns a number cannot say JUST FALSE, so it has a yes/no twin.

§ `Section 2(1) -- when section 2 has effect`

-- The order of the arms is the Act's own: the "where" of s 2(1) first, then "subject to section 3".
-- src:36 | 2.—(1) Where a contract has become impossible of performance or
-- src:37 | been otherwise frustrated, and the parties to the contract have for that
-- src:38 | reason been discharged from the further performance of the contract,
-- src:39 | this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect in relation to that
-- src:40 | contract.
@ref s 2(1) (src:36-40)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated"
        IF NOT (c's `the contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "and the parties to the contract have for that reason been discharged from the further performance of the contract"
        IF NOT (c's `the parties to the contract have for that reason been discharged from the further performance of the contract`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- "subject to section 3": s 3's answer governs (JUST FALSE where s 3 says the Act does not apply; NOTHING where s 3 cannot be applied)
        OTHERWISE `s 3 this Act applies to the contract` c

§ `Section 2(2) -- sums paid or payable`

-- The time bound: the sums are "paid or payable to any party in pursuance of the contract before the time when the parties were so discharged".
-- "In pursuance of the contract" describes the class of sums (a scope limit with no "only"), so a sum outside it answers NOTHING (ruling of 2026-11-05 (c)).
-- "Before the time ... discharged" is a time the text fixes in its own words (ruling of 2026-11-05 (a)), so a sum outside it answers JUST FALSE; fork F2 in NOTES.md records the other reading (NOTHING).
-- src:42 | (2) Subject to subsection (3), all sums paid or payable to any party
-- src:43 | in pursuance of the contract before the time when the parties were so
-- src:44 | discharged (referred to in this Act as the time of discharge) shall, in
-- src:45 | the case of sums so paid, be recoverable from him as money received
-- src:46 | by him for the use of the party by whom the sums were paid, and, in
-- src:47 | the case of sums so payable, cease to be so payable.
@ref s 2(2) (src:42-47)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) a sum paid is recoverable from the party who received it` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "paid or payable to any party in pursuance of the contract": a sum not paid or payable in pursuance of the contract is outside the subsection
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or payable to a party in pursuance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in the case of sums so paid": this limb reaches only a sum that was paid (for a sum payable, see the next function)
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid`) THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a time the text fixes in its own words): "before the time when the parties were so discharged": a sum paid at or after that time is not one the subsection reaches
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or fell payable before the time when the parties were so discharged`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- "Subject to subsection (3)": recoverable unless and to the extent the court allows the recipient to retain it under s 2(3); the amount left is computed below
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:42 | (2) Subject to subsection (3), all sums paid or payable to any party
-- src:43 | in pursuance of the contract before the time when the parties were so
-- src:44 | discharged (referred to in this Act as the time of discharge) shall, in
@ref s 2(2) (src:42-44)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) a sum payable ceases to be payable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "paid or payable to any party in pursuance of the contract": a sum not paid or payable in pursuance of the contract is outside the subsection
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or payable to a party in pursuance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in the case of sums so payable": this limb reaches only a sum that was payable and not paid
        IF sum's `the sum was paid` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a time the text fixes in its own words): "before the time when the parties were so discharged": a sum falling payable at or after that time is not one the subsection reaches
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or fell payable before the time when the parties were so discharged`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- "Subject to subsection (3)": a sum that has ceased to be payable can still be recovered in part by the payee if the court so allows under s 2(3)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 2(5) -- how expenses are estimated`

-- The court's power to include a reasonable sum for overheads and personal work reaches "any expenses incurred by any party to the contract".
-- The sums the court includes are inputs (0 where it includes nothing); the estimate is plain addition.
-- src:82 | (5) In estimating, for the purposes of this section, the amount of any
-- src:83 | expenses incurred by any party to the contract, the court may, without
-- src:84 | prejudice to the generality of this section, include such sum as
-- src:85 | appears to be reasonable in respect of overhead expenses and in
-- src:86 | respect of any work or services performed personally by that party.
@ref s 2(5) (src:82-86)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(5) the court may include a reasonable sum for overhead expenses and for work performed personally` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF exp's `expenses were incurred before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is to include sums in estimating "expenses incurred by any party to the contract"; where none were incurred there is nothing to estimate
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- The estimated expenses: the expenses incurred, plus the sums the court includes for overheads and for personal work.
-- Its yes/no twin is the function above.
-- src:82 | (5) In estimating, for the purposes of this section, the amount of any
-- src:83 | expenses incurred by any party to the contract, the court may, without
-- src:84 | prejudice to the generality of this section, include such sum as
-- src:85 | appears to be reasonable in respect of overhead expenses and in
-- src:86 | respect of any work or services performed personally by that party.
@ref s 2(5) (src:82-86)
GIVEN exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(5) the expenses as estimated` MEANS
    IF exp's `expenses were incurred before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract`
    THEN JUST (  exp's `the amount of those expenses`
               PLUS exp's `the sum the court includes in respect of overhead expenses`
               PLUS exp's `the sum the court includes in respect of work or services performed personally by that party`)
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 2(5) estimates "expenses incurred by any party to the contract"; where none were incurred there is nothing to estimate
        NOTHING

-- The same figure as a plain number, for arithmetic: where no expenses were incurred their amount is 0 (an amount, not a gap).
@ref s 2(5) (src:82-86)
GIVEN exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 2(5) the amount of the expenses as estimated, counting none incurred as 0` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 2(5) the expenses as estimated` exp
    WHEN NOTHING THEN 0
    WHEN JUST e THEN e

§ `Section 2(3) -- the court may let the recipient keep or recover the expenses`

-- Two "if"s: "If the party ... incurred expenses before the time of discharge" and "if it considers it just to do so".  The first is the
-- party's fact, the second the court's own judgment (an input).  The ceiling is the Act's: "not being an amount in excess of the expenses so incurred",
-- the expenses being as estimated under s 2(5).
-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
-- src:60 | any part of the sums so paid or payable, not being an amount in excess
-- src:61 | of the expenses so incurred.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the sums so paid or payable under subsection (2)": a sum outside s 2(2) is outside this subsection
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or payable to a party in pursuance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a time the text fixes in its own words): "the sums so paid or payable under subsection (2)", which are those paid or payable "before the time of discharge": a sum at or after that time is not one this subsection reaches
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or fell payable before the time when the parties were so discharged`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (an "if" that is not met): "If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract"
        IF NOT (exp's `expenses were incurred before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (an "if" that is not met): "if it considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the case"
        IF `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether the court "considers it just" is its judgment, and it has not said
        IF `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The ceiling for ONE sum.  A number cannot say JUST FALSE, so its yes/no twin is the function above.  For the sums paid and payable taken together, see the last section of this module.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the ceiling exists only where section 2 has effect, the sum is one s 2(2) reaches, and the party incurred expenses; see the yes/no twin for which of these fails
        IF NOT (`s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or payable to a party in pursuance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (sum's `the sum was paid or fell payable before the time when the parties were so discharged`) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (exp's `expenses were incurred before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `s 2(5) the expenses as estimated` exp
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST e THEN JUST (min (sum's `the amount of the sum`) e)

-- The amount the court has in fact allowed is within the Act's ceiling.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the amount the court allows` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the amount the court allows is within the limit` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): there is a ceiling only where the s 2(3) power is available at all; see the ceiling function
        IF `s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered` c sum exp EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "not being an amount in excess of the expenses so incurred"
        IF `the amount the court allows` GREATER THAN (`s 2(5) the amount of the expenses as estimated, counting none incurred as 0` exp) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the whole or any part of the sums so paid or payable": an amount above the sums is not any part of them
        IF `the amount the court allows` GREATER THAN (sum's `the amount of the sum`) THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Sums paid: what is left to recover once the court's allowance (if any) is taken off.
-- "Subject to subsection (3)": the whole sum is recoverable unless the court allows the recipient to retain part of it.  Where the s 2(3)
-- power is not available (no expenses, or the court does not consider it just) nothing is retained and the whole sum is recoverable.
-- src:45 | the case of sums so paid, be recoverable from him as money received
-- src:46 | by him for the use of the party by whom the sums were paid, and, in
@ref s 2(2), s 2(3) (src:45-46)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the amount the court allows` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(2) subject to s 2(3) the amount of the sum paid that is recoverable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): this limb reaches only a sum that s 2(2) makes recoverable; see `s 2(2) a sum paid is recoverable from the party who received it`
        IF NOT (`s 2(2) a sum paid is recoverable from the party who received it` c sum EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        IF `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` c sum exp `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST (sum's `the amount of the sum`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether the court allows any of the sum to be retained is its judgment, and it has not said
        IF `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` c sum exp `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the amount the court allows`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): how much the court allows is its judgment, and it has not said
                NOTHING
            WHEN JUST r THEN
                IF `s 2(3) the amount the court allows is within the limit` c sum exp r EQUALS JUST TRUE
                THEN JUST ((sum's `the amount of the sum`) MINUS r)
                ELSE
                    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): an allowance above the s 2(3) ceiling is not one the subsection provides for
                    NOTHING

-- Sums payable: the sum ceases to be payable; what remains payable is what the court allows the payee to recover.
-- src:45 | the case of sums so paid, be recoverable from him as money received
-- src:46 | by him for the use of the party by whom the sums were paid, and, in
-- src:47 | the case of sums so payable, cease to be so payable.
@ref s 2(2), s 2(3) (src:45-47)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the amount the court allows` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(2) subject to s 2(3) the amount of the sum payable that remains payable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): this limb reaches only a sum that s 2(2) makes cease to be payable; see `s 2(2) a sum payable ceases to be payable`
        IF NOT (`s 2(2) a sum payable ceases to be payable` c sum EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        IF `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` c sum exp `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST 0
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether the court allows any of the sum to be recovered is its judgment, and it has not said
        IF `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` c sum exp `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the amount the court allows`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): how much the court allows is its judgment, and it has not said
                NOTHING
            WHEN JUST r THEN
                IF `s 2(3) the amount the court allows is within the limit` c sum exp r EQUALS JUST TRUE
                THEN JUST r
                ELSE
                    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): an allowance above the s 2(3) ceiling is not one the subsection provides for
                    NOTHING

§ `Section 2(4) -- a valuable benefit`

-- A "Where" (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): when no party has obtained a valuable benefit by reason of anything the other party did in
-- performing the contract, or the benefit was not obtained before the time of discharge, the answer is JUST FALSE.  The parenthesis
-- "(other than a payment of money to which subsection (2) applies)" is part of what the "Where" describes, so a payment to which s 2(2) applies is a "where" not met: JUST FALSE (QE; NOTES.md has the other reading, NOTHING).
-- src:63 | (4) Where any party to the contract has, by reason of anything done
-- src:64 | by any other party to the contract in, or for the purpose of, the
-- src:65 | performance of the contract, obtained a valuable benefit (other than a
-- src:66 | payment of money to which subsection (2) applies) before the time of
-- src:67 | discharge, there shall be recoverable from him by that other party
-- src:68 | such sum (if any), not exceeding the value of that benefit to the party
-- src:69 | obtaining it, as the court considers just, having regard to all the
-- src:70 | circumstances of the case and, in particular —
@ref s 2(4) (src:63-70)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      b IS A `Benefit facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "a valuable benefit (other than a payment of money to which subsection (2) applies)": a payment of money to which s 2(2) applies is not the benefit s 2(4) speaks of; it is dealt with by s 2(2) and s 2(3)
        IF b's `the benefit is a payment of money to which section 2(2) applies` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "Where any party to the contract has, by reason of anything done by any other party to the contract in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, obtained a valuable benefit"
        IF NOT (b's `a party to the contract has obtained a valuable benefit by reason of anything done by any other party to the contract in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "obtained a valuable benefit ... before the time of discharge"
        IF NOT (b's `the benefit was obtained before the time of discharge`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- "there shall be recoverable from him by that other party such sum (if any)": a sum, which may be nil, is recoverable; how much is the court's judgment
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The ceiling: "not exceeding the value of that benefit to the party obtaining it".  Its yes/no twin is the function above.
@ref s 2(4) (src:63-70)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      b IS A `Benefit facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(4) the most that is recoverable for the valuable benefit` MEANS
    IF `s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` c b EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST (b's `the value of the benefit to the party obtaining it`)
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the ceiling belongs to the sum s 2(4) makes recoverable; see the yes/no twin for why none is
        NOTHING

-- The sum the court in fact considers just is within the ceiling.
@ref s 2(4) (src:63-70)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      b IS A `Benefit facts`
      `the sum the court considers just` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(4) the sum the court considers just is within the limit` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the ceiling belongs to the sum s 2(4) makes recoverable; see the yes/no twin for why none is
        IF NOT (`s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` c b EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "not exceeding the value of that benefit to the party obtaining it"
        IF `the sum the court considers just` GREATER THAN (b's `the value of the benefit to the party obtaining it`) THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- (a): the amount of the benefited party's expenses (estimated as s 2(5) allows), "including any sums paid or payable by him to any other
-- party in pursuance of the contract and retained or recoverable by that party under subsection (3)".  A matter the court has regard to,
-- not a ceiling.  Where no expenses were incurred, only the sums retained or recoverable count.
-- src:72 | (a) the amount of any expenses incurred before the time of
-- src:73 | discharge by the benefited party in, or for the purpose of,
-- src:74 | the performance of the contract, including any sums paid
-- src:75 | or payable by him to any other party in pursuance of the
-- src:76 | contract and retained or recoverable by that party under
-- src:77 | subsection (3); and
@ref s 2(4)(a) (src:72-77)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      b IS A `Benefit facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(4)(a) the amount of the benefited party's expenses that the court has regard to` MEANS
    IF `s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` c b EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST (  (`s 2(5) the amount of the expenses as estimated, counting none incurred as 0` exp)
               PLUS b's `the sums paid or payable by the benefited party to any other party in pursuance of the contract and retained or recoverable by that party under section 2(3)`)
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (a) is a matter for the court under s 2(4); it arises only where s 2(4) makes a sum recoverable
        NOTHING

-- The matters the court is to have regard to: "all the circumstances of the case and, in particular -- (a) ... (b) ...".
-- src:79 | (b) the effect, in relation to that benefit, of the circumstances
-- src:80 | giving rise to the frustration of the contract.
@ref s 2(4)(b) (src:79-80)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      b IS A `Benefit facts`
      m IS A `Matter for section 2(4)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(4) the court is to have regard to this matter` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): the court has these matters to regard only where s 2(4) makes a sum recoverable
        IF `s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` c b EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether s 2(4) makes a sum recoverable cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(4) a sum is recoverable from the party who obtained a valuable benefit` c b EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER m
            WHEN `the expenses incurred before the time of discharge by the benefited party (paragraph (a))` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `the effect, in relation to that benefit, of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract (paragraph (b))` THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN `some other circumstance of the case` THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "all the circumstances of the case": what is not a circumstance of the case is not within the instruction
            WHEN `something that is not a circumstance of the case` THEN NOTHING

§ `Section 2(6) -- insurance money`

-- Two questions on one subsection.  The bar: "the court shall not take into account any sums which have ... become payable to that party
-- under any contract of insurance unless there was an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated contract or by or
-- under any enactment".  "In considering" is read as "when considering" (ruling of 2026-11-05 (c), counted as "where": JUST FALSE when the
-- court is not considering a recovery or retention under section 2).  The class of sums is a scope limit (NOTHING outside it).
-- When the "unless" is met the BAR does not apply (JUST FALSE), and the PERMISSION question answers NOTHING (a lifted bar grants nothing; QA).
-- src:88 | (6) In considering whether any sum ought to be recovered or
-- src:89 | retained under this section by any party to the contract, the court shall
-- src:90 | not take into account any sums which have, by reason of the
-- src:91 | circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become
-- src:92 | payable to that party under any contract of insurance unless there was
-- src:93 | an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated
-- src:94 | contract or by or under any enactment.
@ref s 2(6) (src:88-94)
GIVEN i IS AN `Insurance facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(6) the court shall not take the insurance sums into account` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "when" read as a "where", that is not met): "In considering whether any sum ought to be recovered or retained under this section by any party to the contract"
        IF NOT (i's `the court is considering whether any sum ought to be recovered or retained under section 2 by the party`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any sums which have, by reason of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become payable to that party under any contract of insurance": other sums are outside the bar
        IF NOT (i's `sums have, by reason of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become payable to the party under a contract of insurance`) THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (an "unless" that is met: the bar does not apply): "unless there was an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated contract"
        IF i's `there was an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated contract` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (an "unless" that is met: the bar does not apply): "unless there was an obligation to insure imposed ... by or under any enactment"
        IF i's `there was an obligation to insure imposed by or under an enactment` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 2(6) (src:88-94)
GIVEN i IS AN `Insurance facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(6) the court may take the insurance sums into account` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of the court considering a recovery or retention under section 2; outside it the Act says nothing about insurance money
        IF NOT (i's `the court is considering whether any sum ought to be recovered or retained under section 2 by the party`) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any sums which have, by reason of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become payable to that party under any contract of insurance": other sums are outside the bar
        IF NOT (i's `sums have, by reason of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become payable to the party under a contract of insurance`) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): an "unless" that is met lifts the bar ("unless there was an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated contract"), and a lifted bar grants no permission
        IF i's `there was an obligation to insure imposed by an express term of the frustrated contract` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): an "unless" that is met lifts the bar ("or by or under any enactment"), and a lifted bar grants no permission
        IF i's `there was an obligation to insure imposed by or under an enactment` THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the court shall not take into account any sums which have, by reason of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become payable to that party under any contract of insurance"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 2(7) -- a benefit conferred on someone else`

-- A "Where" (JUST FALSE when not met) and a power "if ... it considers it just" (the court's judgment, an input).  "Whether a party to
-- the contract or not" has one answer: the recipient's being a party never changes the result (tested both ways).
-- src:96 | (7) Where any person has assumed obligations under the contract in
-- src:97 | consideration of the conferring of a benefit by any other party to the
-- src:98 | contract upon any other person, whether a party to the contract or not,
-- src:99 | the court may, if in all the circumstances of the case it considers it just
-- src:100 | to do so, treat for the purposes of subsection (4) any benefit so
-- src:103 | conferred as a benefit obtained by the person who has assumed those
-- src:104 | obligations.
@ref s 2(7) (src:96-104)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      o IS AN `Obligor facts`
      `the court considers it just, in all the circumstances of the case, to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(7) the court may treat the benefit conferred on another person as a benefit obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met, carried from s 2(1)): "Where a contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated, and the parties ... have for that reason been discharged ..., this section shall, subject to section 3, have effect"
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether section 2 has effect cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "Where any person has assumed obligations under the contract"
        IF NOT (o's `a person has assumed obligations under the contract`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "in consideration of the conferring of a benefit by any other party to the contract upon any other person"
        IF NOT (o's `the obligations were assumed in consideration of the conferring of a benefit by another party to the contract upon another person`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (an "if" that is not met): "if in all the circumstances of the case it considers it just to do so"
        IF `the court considers it just, in all the circumstances of the case, to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether the court "considers it just" is its judgment, and it has not said
        IF `the court considers it just, in all the circumstances of the case, to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE


§ `Section 2(3) -- the sums paid and the sums payable, taken together`

-- The ceiling of s 2(3) is one ceiling for "the sums so paid or payable under subsection (2)" taken together: the court may allow the party to
-- retain or recover "the whole or any part of the sums ... not being an amount in excess of the expenses so incurred".  The functions above
-- take ONE sum, so on their own they would allow each sum its own ceiling (2,000 paid and 3,000 payable against expenses of 4,000 would allow
-- 2,000 and 3,000, which is 5,000 in all, where the Act allows 4,000).  The functions below take all the sums together and are the Act's cap;
-- the single-sum functions above are kept as they are, for one sum.
-- Only the sums s 2(2) reaches are counted: those paid or payable in pursuance of the contract before the time of discharge.
-- ORDER: the Act does not say in what order the sums paid and the sums payable are counted against the ceiling, nor whether the allowance
-- is shared between a part "retained" and a part "recovered".  The total is a sum, so the order of the list changes nothing, and these
-- functions return only the aggregate amount the court may allow (fork F6 in NOTES.md).
-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
-- src:60 | any part of the sums so paid or payable, not being an amount in excess
-- src:61 | of the expenses so incurred.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN sums IS A LIST OF `Sum facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`s 2(3) the amounts of the sums that s 2(2) reaches` MEANS
    map (GIVEN u YIELD u's `the amount of the sum`)
        (filter (GIVEN t YIELD (t's `the sum was paid or payable to a party in pursuance of the contract`) AND (t's `the sum was paid or fell payable before the time when the parties were so discharged`)) sums)

-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
-- src:60 | any part of the sums so paid or payable, not being an amount in excess
-- src:61 | of the expenses so incurred.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN sums IS A LIST OF `Sum facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(3) the total of the sums taken together` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 2(3) the amounts of the sums that s 2(2) reaches` sums
    WHEN EMPTY THEN
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the sums so paid or payable under subsection (2)": none of the sums given is one s 2(2) reaches, so there is no total
        NOTHING
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN JUST (x PLUS (sum rest))

-- The ceiling over the sums taken together: the smaller of their total and the expenses as estimated under s 2(5).
-- Its yes/no twin is `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums`, asked of any one of the sums.
-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
-- src:60 | any part of the sums so paid or payable, not being an amount in excess
-- src:61 | of the expenses so incurred.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sums IS A LIST OF `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered from the sums taken together` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the ceiling exists only where section 2 has effect and the party incurred expenses; the single-sum ceiling above says the same
        IF NOT (`s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` c EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (exp's `expenses were incurred before the time of discharge in, or for the purpose of, the performance of the contract`) THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `s 2(3) the total of the sums taken together` sums
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST total THEN
                CONSIDER `s 2(5) the expenses as estimated` exp
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
                WHEN JUST e THEN JUST (min total e)

-- The amount the court has in fact allowed from the sums taken together is within the Act's ceiling.
-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
-- src:60 | any part of the sums so paid or payable, not being an amount in excess
-- src:61 | of the expenses so incurred.
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-61)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sums IS A LIST OF `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the amount the court allows` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the amount the court allows from the sums taken together is within the limit` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): there is a ceiling only where the s 2(3) power is available at all; see the ceiling function
        IF `s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered from the sums taken together` c sums exp EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "not being an amount in excess of the expenses so incurred"
        IF `the amount the court allows` GREATER THAN (`s 2(5) the amount of the expenses as estimated, counting none incurred as 0` exp) THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered from the sums taken together` c sums exp
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            WHEN JUST k THEN
                BRANCH
                    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the whole or any part of the sums so paid or payable": an amount above the total of the sums is not any part of them
                    IF `the amount the court allows` GREATER THAN k THEN NOTHING
                    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
